    description: "ADR-0023 §6 Phase 4 (Schema bump + deprecation removal, ~1-2 days estimated by the ADR). Once Phase 3 (sibling: WI-mokam-jalig-fuhab-gifug-sigij-gotar-sisig-zufoz) has unified producers — every endpoint_shape value in the canonical registry has zero production emit sites — Phase 4 removes the values from the registry, bumps the schema version per ADR-0014's schema-change protocol, and gives external consumers a deprecation grace window before the values hard-fail.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nADR-0023 (docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md, currently Draft) classifies edge_type values on the relationship / endpoint_shape / pending_classification axis. PR #3459 landed the canonical registry; PR #3462 added the pre-commit drift linter; PR #3464 added four missing endpoint_shape values plus one reclassification (query_references); PR #3468 (ADR-0024) extracted the abstract axis-declaration template.\n\nPhase 4 is the final cleanup pass. It assumes:\n- Phase 2 (sibling: WI-sahab-fatoz-zosaj-nopuk-nozon-dosud-mogal-juhim) has migrated all consumers to (edge_type, dst.kind) queries.\n- Phase 3 (sibling: WI-mokam-jalig) has unified all producers — no analyzer emits any endpoint_shape value.\n- Bakeoff validation has confirmed no regressions in slice / ranking / sketch output (the awaits_bakeoff_validation tags on Phase 2/3 PRs have cleared).\n\nTHE MIGRATION SHAPE (per ADR-0023 §6 Phase 4, transcribed verbatim):\n\n> Bump docs/schema.json per ADR-0014's schema-change protocol. The deprecated edge-type strings remain valid for one minor version (so external consumers can adapt), then hard-fail in the version after.\n\nThis splits naturally into two sub-phases, possibly two minor releases apart:\n\nPhase 4a — Mark as deprecated in schema (single minor bump):\n- Annotate the deprecated values in the schema (e.g., via a x-deprecated: true extension key on each value, paired with x-deprecated-since and x-replacement metadata pointing at the canonical type + dst.kind composition).\n- Keep the values valid in the schema enum so external consumers do not immediately break.\n- Bump SCHEMA_VERSION minor (e.g., 0.2.x → 0.3.0) since this is a semantically meaningful change for consumers.\n- Communicate to external consumers via CHANGELOG with a clear migration timeline (dual-validity window length, intended removal version).\n\nPhase 4b — Hard-fail (one minor version after 4a):\n- Remove the deprecated values from EDGE_TYPES (the canonical registry in hypergumbo_core.edge_types).\n- Regenerate docs/schema.json — values disappear from the enum.\n- Regenerate docs/concept-axes.md — endpoint_shape section becomes empty (or its preamble notes 'all values migrated; section retained as a marker that the axis previously held deprecation candidates').\n- Bump SCHEMA_VERSION minor again.\n- The drift property test (find_axis_drift in hypergumbo_core.edge_types, used by both the in-tree test and scripts/check-edge-type-drift) now treats any remaining consumer-side reference as drift (Phase 2 should have caught these, but Phase 4b catches any stragglers).\n\nEndpoint_shape values currently slated for removal (after Phase 3 completes; transcribed from the registry's endpoint_shape section as of PR #3464):\n- imports_module\n- imports_component\n- model_reference\n- type_ref\n- query_references\n- renders_component\n- cgo_bridge, ffi_bridge, napi_bridge, wasm_bridge, wasm_load, bridge_invokes (bridge / FFI family)\n- ipc_calls, ipc_event (after IPC family audit determines canonical landings)\n- native_bridge\n- message_send, message_receive, websocket_message, websocket_connection, grpc_calls, http_calls, graphql_calls, message_queue, script_src, base_image, kernel_launch\n\nDEPENDENCIES:\n- HARD: Phase 3 (WI-mokam-jalig) must complete first — every endpoint_shape value must have zero production emit sites before removal.\n- HARD: bakeoff validation of Phase 2/3 must be confirmed (awaits_bakeoff_validation tags on those PRs must clear) before Phase 4b removes the deprecated values.\n- HARD: ADR-0023 should be Accepted (not Draft) by Phase 4b — removing schema enum values is a hard contract change with downstream consumers.\n- SOFT: per-family audits for dispatch, publish, and IPC families must resolve canonical names; their corresponding endpoint_shape (or pending_classification) values can only be removed after.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Phase 4a: schema.json gains x-deprecated annotations on the to-be-removed values; SCHEMA_VERSION bumped per ADR-0014's schema-change protocol; CHANGELOG entry names the deprecation window and intended removal version.\n- Phase 4b: every Phase-3-migrated endpoint_shape value is removed from EDGE_TYPES and schema.json; concept-axes.md regenerates cleanly; drift property test passes; check-edge-type-drift CLI passes.\n- After Phase 4b, the next item — drift-linter-tightening (separate sibling tracker item) — becomes feasible because the registry no longer contains endpoint_shape 'exceptions to the axis principle.'\n\nCONSIDERATIONS:\n- ADR-0014 (Generalized Symbol Identity) is the cited governance for schema bumps. Phase 4 should follow its instructions for additive vs. breaking changes if applicable.\n- The dual-validity window length ('one minor version') is a judgment call. May need to be longer if downstream tooling or external agents take longer than a single release to adapt. Surface this in the Phase 4a PR description for explicit human review.\n- This is exactly the kind of breaking-change PR that warrants extra care: bakeoff confirmation in advance, possibly tagging stop-the-line if external consumer queries surface during the dual-validity window.\n\nSIBLINGS:\n- WI-sahab-fatoz-zosaj-nopuk-nozon-dosud-mogal-juhim — ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2 (must be done).\n- WI-mokam-jalig-fuhab-gifug-sigij-gotar-sisig-zufoz — ADR-0023 §6 Phase 3 (must be done).\n- ADR-0023 advancement from Draft to Accepted — separate item; Phase 4b should not land while ADR is Draft.\n- Drift-linter-tightening (axis-principle enforcement, not just registry membership) — separate item; gated on Phase 4b completion.\n- Per-family audit items for dispatch, publish, IPC families — separate; Phase 4b's family-touching subset waits on each.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced as loose-end #5 in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026). ADR-0023's migration plan named Phase 4 in the ADR text but the implementation never started; tracker captures the work so it does not slip."  # 0b3e
